<ui version="4.0">
 <class>ExtendedSliderUi</class>
 <widget class="QWidget" name="ExtendedSliderUi">
  <property name="geometry">
   <rect>
    <x>0</x>
    <y>0</y>
    <width>519</width>
    <height>138</height>
   </rect>
  </property>
  <property name="windowTitle">
   <string>Form</string>
  </property>
  <!--
    Eighteen of these stack up in the tool dock (three in Raise/Lower alone), so the layout
    metrics here are what decide how much of the panel is chrome. Four things were costing
    height or legibility for nothing.

    First, every layout took the Fusion defaults (9px margins, 6px spacing). A control that is
    already inside a group box, itself inside a panel, does not need a third 9px inset; the
    parent supplies the gutter. Margins are zeroed and the row gap is set explicitly.

    Second, the slider and the pressure bar each sat in their own single item QHBoxLayout.
    Those wrappers were pure overhead, and the pressureBar one was actively harmful: the bar is
    hidden unless a tablet is driving the value (see ExtendedSlider.cpp), and while Qt skips a
    hidden WIDGET in a layout, a nested LAYOUT holding it is not empty, so it kept claiming its
    margins and spacing on every one of the eighteen instances. Both widgets are now direct
    children of the vertical layout, which makes the hidden bar genuinely free.

    Third, the slider took whatever height the style's slider thickness happened to be - around
    22px under Fusion, and none of it used. The band is pinned as a MINIMUM as well as a maximum,
    so the row height is this file's decision rather than the style's. That was the single
    biggest saving on the panel, before the row gap below dropped from 3 to 2.

    THE BAND IS 20px AND IT IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN. It is Design::SLIDER_BAND, and the whole
    derivation is on that constant in DesignTokens.hpp; in one line, QSS width/height size the
    CONTENT rect and the border adds, QStyleSheetStyle places the handle inside the groove's
    contents rect expanded by the handle's negative margins, and the WIDGET band then clips it:

        handle box = declared QSS size + 2 x border width
        handle box = groove box + 2 x |cross-axis margin|
        grabbable  = min(handle box, band), centred

    The theme draws a 6px groove with the handle margin at -7px, so 6 + 7 + 7 = 20 is the box
    and a band under 20 clips the grip into a circle with a flat top and bottom.

    BOTH NUMBERS BELOW HAVE TO MOVE TOGETHER. A maximumHeight clamps the widget below any QSS
    min-height, so changing only the minimum leaves the grip clipped to the maximum, and changing
    only the maximum leaves the layout free to give it less.

    THIS IS THE CHANGE AN EARLIER PASS MADE AND THEN REVERTED, AND THE REASON TO REDO IT IS NOT
    THE REASON IT WAS MADE. That pass widened the band while the sheet's handle box stayed at
    16, and it argued that the band sets the hit target because Qt derives SC_SliderHandle from
    the WIDGET height. That argument is true of an UNSTYLED slider and false of this one, and
    the revert measured it with a standalone Qt 5.15.2 probe that loads the shipped sheet, puts
    a 212px slider at 50 percent and sweeps the pointer pixel by pixel counting the positions
    QStyle::hitTestComplexControl(CC_Slider, ...) reports as SC_SliderHandle - the exact call
    QSlider::mousePressEvent uses to choose between dragging and page-stepping:

      sheet                    band   handle rect       pixels that GRAB the handle
      6px tick, 4px groove      16     6x16 @(103,0)     96 =  6 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     14    16x16 @(98,-1)    224 = 16 x 14  (grip clipped by band)
      16px grip, 6px groove     16    16x16 @(98,0)     256 = 16 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     18    16x16 @(98,1)     256 = 16 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     20    16x16 @(98,2)     256 = 16 x 16
      16px grip, 6px groove     22    16x16 @(98,3)     256 = 16 x 16
      none, QWindowsVistaStyle  16    16x16 @(98,0)     256 = 16 x 16
      none, QWindowsVistaStyle  20    16x20 @(98,0)     320 = 16 x 20

    Read the middle four rows again: with a sheet loaded the handle rect is 16x16 at EVERY band
    from 16 up, because QStyleSheetStyle sizes it from the QSS box and centres it. So widening
    the band alone bought exactly zero grabbable pixels and charged 4px x 18 instances for it -
    which is why it was reverted, correctly.

    What is different now is that the BOX moves with the band: the sheet's grip goes to a 20px
    box in the same pass, and by the same table a 20px box in a 20px band is 20 x 20 = 400
    grabbable pixels against the 256 those rows all report. The cost is unchanged and honest -
    +4px x 18 instances, +12px of dock height in the densest panel, Raise/Lower with three of
    these - but this time it buys 56 percent more hit target on the control that sets brush
    radius and speed, instead of nothing.

    Fourth, the value was left-aligned, so in a column of three sliders the digits started at
    three different x positions and the eye had nothing to scan down. The spinbox is now
    right-aligned. That is the whole fix and it costs no width: the row is label, expanding
    spacer, spinbox, fixed-width button, so the RIGHT edge of every spinbox in a column is
    already flush regardless of how wide each spinbox is, and right-aligned text puts the digits
    on that shared edge. A minimum width was tried here first and does not work - the spinbox
    size hints are 89px at max 1000, 95px at max 10000 and 71px at max 1, so any floor small
    enough to fit the 250px dock is below the widest of them and aligns nothing. Alignment does
    not touch the range, the step, the decimals or the emitted value.

    The pencil button is the tablet-pressure menu, NOT a mask or curve picker - read
    ExtendedSlider.cpp, the popup it opens holds "Use Tablet" and a sensitivity slider. It had
    no tooltip at all, which is most of why it reads as mysterious. It is now flat (the theme's
    QPushButton:flat is transparent until hovered) so it stops carrying the same visual weight
    as the number beside it, and 20px rather than 22px - which is the slider band exactly, so the
    two rows of this widget agree on one height instead of the button setting the taller one.

    No widget was renamed, removed or reordered; ExtendedSlider.cpp addresses the same five
    objects and none of its ranges or connections changed.
  -->
  <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="verticalLayout">
   <property name="spacing">
    <number>2</number>
   </property>
   <property name="leftMargin">
    <number>0</number>
